1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Flip a fair coin four times.

2
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:06,420
After each heads, check the next flip: here, one of the two is heads.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:10,400
Do it for all sixteen sequences and average the shares.

4
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:11,810
What do you get?

5
00:00:13,766 --> 00:00:17,256
One half feels obvious, since a coin has no memory.

6
00:00:17,636 --> 00:00:25,706
In nineteen eighty five, a famous study ran this same check on basketball shots, and called the hot hand an illusion.

7
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:27,460
Heads are hits.

8
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:32,010
A hot shooter beats their usual rate; a random one should match it.

9
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:37,116
So first, test the test on a shooter we know is random: a fair coin.

10
00:00:37,236 --> 00:00:41,186
Four flips can land sixteen ways, all equally likely.

11
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:44,773
This row checks three flips, and two of them are heads.

12
00:00:45,373 --> 00:00:47,343
Score every row the same way.

13
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:52,290
Two rows never show a heads before the last flip, so they get no score.

14
00:00:52,633 --> 00:00:55,473
Drop the other fourteen scores onto the line.

15
00:00:55,853 --> 00:01:01,253
Their average is seventeen out of forty two: about forty point five percent.

16
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:06,660
On a perfectly fair coin, the test reads cold, by nine and a half points.

17
00:01:07,466 --> 00:01:09,246
Yet no flip is biased.

18
00:01:09,846 --> 00:01:14,096
Gather all twenty four checked flips: twelve heads, twelve tails.

19
00:01:14,216 --> 00:01:15,496
Exactly half.

20
00:01:15,833 --> 00:01:22,723
The skew comes from scoring row by row, because each row gets one vote, however many flips it checked.

21
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:27,706
And checked heads come in crowds, since a heads gets the next flip checked too.

22
00:01:28,086 --> 00:01:33,306
This row's three heads share a single vote, while this lone tails gets a whole one.

23
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,120
Twelve heads crowd into eight rows.

24
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:38,820
Twelve tails spread across eleven.

25
00:01:39,370 --> 00:01:43,350
Line up the votes, and heads hold seventeen out of forty two.

26
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:45,390
That's one vote per row.

27
00:01:45,690 --> 00:01:49,180
Count all the flips together first, and you get one half.

28
00:01:50,066 --> 00:01:51,716
Tails crowd the same way.

29
00:01:52,096 --> 00:01:55,856
Swap heads and tails, and the strip flips over one half.

30
00:01:56,236 --> 00:02:01,036
So after a tails, the test reads hot: twenty five out of forty two.

31
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:06,880
Subtract the two averages, and a fair coin shows a gap of minus nineteen points.

32
00:02:07,566 --> 00:02:17,626
The nineteen eighty five study ran the same comparison on twenty six Cornell players: up to a hundred shots each, from where they'd hit about half.

33
00:02:18,006 --> 00:02:21,196
After three hits, versus after three misses.

34
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:27,133
For a random shooter, the same crowding pulls those to forty six and fifty four percent.

35
00:02:27,253 --> 00:02:33,253
Miller and Sanjurjo's twenty eighteen paper puts the gap at about minus eight points.

36
00:02:33,853 --> 00:02:35,763
Longer streaks grow the bias.

37
00:02:36,663 --> 00:02:38,313
Longer records shrink it,

38
00:02:38,813 --> 00:02:40,763
but a hundred shots isn't long.

39
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:47,640
Cornell's players hit forty nine percent after three hits, and forty five after three misses.

40
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:51,000
The study took that plus four to mean no hot hand.

41
00:02:51,366 --> 00:02:54,446
Measured from minus eight, it's twelve points hot.

42
00:02:55,046 --> 00:03:00,596
That's evidence for a hot hand in a gym, not proof it survives defenders in a game.

43
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,530
One half is what a fair coin does.

44
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:08,130
Seventeen out of forty two is what this test sees.

45
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:10,100
A fifty percent shooter

46
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,550
who averages one half on this test

47
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:14,330
is already running hot.

